.TH ALESS 1 "2026-11-02" "aless 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
aless \- JSON, YAML, TOML, CSV, XML, INI, Markdown and more as one tree, in a
jless\-style terminal viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
aless [OPTIONS] [FILE]...
.br
aless [OPTIONS] DIRECTORY
.br
<command> | aless [OPTIONS]
.PP
\-h prints a summary of the options, and \-\-help this whole reference.
.SH DESCRIPTION
aless reads JSON, JSON Lines, JSON5, JSONC, jsonic, YAML, TOML, INI, CSV, TSV,
XML, ZON, Markdown, RSS or Atom feeds, CSS, Protocol Buffers\(aq .proto files,
PGN chess games, C source, grammars in ABNF, EBNF or GBNF, expressions and
semantic versions, and any line\-oriented format an ABNF grammar describes, as
one tree, and shows it in a jless\-style viewer in the terminal, with tabs,
search, a source view and a watch mode that reloads a changed file and keeps
your place.
.SS "The viewer"
aless is a viewer for the terminal. Each FILE opens in a tab of its own, as a
tree to fold, search and copy from, and a directory opens in the file explorer.
With no FILE, aless reads standard input when it is not a terminal, and
otherwise explores the current directory. Every file is watched: when it
changes on disk, aless reads it again and keeps your place. A file that does
not parse opens as the parser\(aqs report, with the line and column where the
parse stopped.
.PP
aless refuses to start without a terminal to draw on and read keys from: when
standard output is a pipe or a file, when there is no terminal to open, or when
TERM is dumb. It refuses before reading any input, with one line on standard
error and the status 2. Only \-h, \-\-help, \-V and \-\-generate write to
standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Input options"
.TP 7
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kind\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
Read every input as FORMAT, whatever its extension says: a format\(aqs name
(json, jsonl, jsonic, jsonc, json5, yaml, toml, ini, csv, tsv, xml, zon,
markdown, feed, css, proto, pgn, c, abnf, ebnf, gbnf, expr, semver or text),
one of its extensions (yml, md, ndjson), or a \-\-grammar NAME. Standard input
is read as JSON unless this says otherwise. \-\-format is another name for this
option.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-grammar\fR \fINAME=FILE\fR
Read every file whose extension or whole name is NAME, in any case (x.hosts,
/etc/hosts), with the ABNF grammar in FILE. NAME is then a format, which \-k
and :format take and the status bar shows; NAME,NAME2=FILE gives it two names,
and the option repeats. FILE is read within \-\-max\-size and compiled within
\-\-timeout, before any input is read. See CUSTOM GRAMMARS.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-grammar\-expr\fR \fINAME=ABNF\fR
As \-\-grammar, with the grammar\(aqs text on the command line: everything
after the first =.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-max\-size\fR \fISIZE\fR
Refuse an input larger than SIZE, a \-\-grammar file included (default 64M; 0
for no limit). SIZE is bytes, or a whole number with K, M or G, in 1024s. A
file over the limit opens in a tab that says so and names the size that would
read it; a grammar file over it is a usage error. A parse needs about 40 bytes
of memory for each byte of input.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a parse, or a \-\-grammar compile, that runs longer than SECONDS (2.5,
90s, 2m; default none; 0 for no limit). A parse that is stopped opens in a tab
that says how far it got; a compile that is stopped is a usage error. A parse
runs at about a megabyte a second. See LARGE INPUTS AND LIMITS.
.SS "Viewer options"
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-watch\fR
Do not reload a file when it changes on disk. W or :watch on starts watching a
tab inside aless.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-watch\fR
Reload a file when it changes, keeping your place (the default).
.TP 7
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-mode\fR \fIMODE\fR
Start in data mode (the default), the tree without its punctuation, or in line
mode, every line as JSON prints it; m switches between them.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
Open only the first N levels when a file opens: a container N levels below the
root starts folded, and so does everything in it, so \-\-depth 0 folds the root
(by default every container is open). :depth N does the same inside aless.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-indent\fR \fIN\fR
Indent the tree N spaces a level (default 2, at most 16); < and > change it
inside aless.
.TP 7
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-line\-numbers\fR
Number the rows on screen, as a count before g or G takes them.
.TP 7
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-no\-line\-numbers\fR
Do not number the rows, undoing an \-n before it.
.TP 7
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-relative\-line\-numbers\fR
Number each row by its distance from the focused row, the count j or k needs to
reach it. With \-n as well, the focused row shows its own number.
.TP 7
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-no\-relative\-line\-numbers\fR
Do not number the rows by distance, undoing an \-r before it.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-scrolloff\fR \fIN\fR
Keep N rows between the focused row and the top or the bottom of the screen
(default 3).
.TP 7
\fB\-\-ascii\fR
Draw the fold markers as v and >, for a font without the triangles aless draws
by default.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR, \fB\-\-no\-colour\fR
Draw without colour, as when NO_COLOR is set.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR
Leave the mouse to the terminal, for its own selection, rather than take its
wheel and clicks.
.SS "Other options"
.TP 7
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a summary of the options (\-h), or this reference (\-\-help).
.TP 7
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version: aless 0.6.0.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-generate\fR \fIWHAT\fR
Print a file written from this reference: man, the man page (aless.1), or
complete\-bash, complete\-zsh, complete\-fish or complete\-powershell, that
shell\(aqs completions. See FILES.
.SH "INPUT AND FORMATS"
FILE is a path, or \- for standard input. Options end at \-\-, after which
every argument is a FILE, and a long option\(aqs value follows it or is
attached with = (\-\-kind=yaml).
.PP
A file\(aqs format comes from its extension, or from its whole name when that
is a \-\-grammar NAME (/etc/hosts), and \-k FORMAT overrides it for every
input. Inside aless, :format FORMAT reads a tab again as FORMAT, and :open PATH
FORMAT opens a file as one. A file whose extension no format claims is read as
plain text, an array of its lines.
.PP
Keys keep their order in the source. A number keeps its spelling where JSON
spells it the same way: 1.0, 1e21 and an integer beyond 2\(ha53 are shown,
searched, copied and written by :w as the source writes them. A spelling JSON
has no syntax for (0x1F, 1_000, +1) gives the 64\-bit float it stands for, and
the source view (s) shows the spelling. The formats, with the extensions that
imply each:
.TP 10
\fBjson\fR
json geojson har jsonld webmanifest
.TP 10
\fBjsonl\fR
jsonl ndjson
.TP 10
\fBjsonic\fR
jsonic
.TP 10
\fBjsonc\fR
jsonc (trailing commas accepted)
.TP 10
\fBjson5\fR
json5
.TP 10
\fByaml\fR
yaml yml
.TP 10
\fBtoml\fR
toml
.TP 10
\fBini\fR
ini cfg conf cnf
.TP 10
\fBcsv\fR
csv (records keyed by the header row)
.TP 10
\fBtsv\fR
tsv tab (as csv)
.TP 10
\fBxml\fR
xml svg xhtml xsd xsl xslt plist
.TP 10
\fBzon\fR
zon
.TP 10
\fBmarkdown\fR
md markdown (its syntax tree)
.TP 10
\fBfeed\fR
rss atom (normalised to an Atom shape)
.TP 10
\fBcss\fR
css (its syntax tree)
.TP 10
\fBproto\fR
proto (its FileDescriptorProto, as protoc writes one in JSON)
.TP 10
\fBpgn\fR
pgn (chess games, each its tags and moves)
.TP 10
\fBc\fR
c h (its concrete syntax tree, every token with its place)
.TP 10
\fBabnf\fR
abnf (the grammar spec its compiler writes, the three notations\(aq one schema)
.TP 10
\fBebnf\fR
ebnf (the grammar spec its compiler writes, the three notations\(aq one schema)
.TP 10
\fBgbnf\fR
gbnf (the grammar spec its compiler writes, the three notations\(aq one schema)
.TP 10
\fBexpr\fR
none, \-k expr (jsonic with arithmetic, an operation the list of its operator
and terms)
.TP 10
\fBsemver\fR
none, \-k semver (a version: its major, minor, patch, prerelease and build)
.TP 10
\fBtext\fR
txt text log (and any file no format claims)
.SH "KEYS"
The keys are jless\(aqs. F1 or :help inside aless lists every key and command;
these are the ones used most:
.TP 11
\fBj k\fR
down and up a row; a count before a key repeats it (3j)
.TP 11
\fBh l\fR
fold and unfold, or go to the parent and the first child
.TP 11
\fBJ K\fR
the next and the previous sibling
.TP 11
\fBg G\fR
the first and the last row; Ng goes to row N
.TP 11
\fBC\-d C\-u\fR
half a page down and up
.TP 11
\fBSpace\fR
fold or unfold the focused container
.TP 11
\fBc e\fR
fold and unfold the focused node and its siblings
.TP 11
\fB/ ?\fR
search forward and backward; n and N go to the next and the previous match
.TP 11
\fByy yp yq\fR
copy the value, its path, or its path as jq writes it
.TP 11
\fBm\fR
switch between data mode and line mode
.TP 11
\fBs\fR
the source view, at the focused node\(aqs line
.TP 11
\fB!\fR
the parse error report, full size
.TP 11
\fBTab S\-Tab\fR
the next and the previous tab
.TP 11
\fB:\fR
a command: :open PATH, :format FORMAT, :w FILE, :help
.TP 11
\fBq\fR
close the tab, quitting with the last one; C\-c quits
.SH "CUSTOM GRAMMARS"
\-\-grammar NAME=FILE reads every file whose extension or whole name is NAME,
in any case, with the ABNF grammar in FILE (RFC 5234, compiled by tabnas/abnf),
and \-k NAME reads any input with it, standard input included. Comments on a
rule say what it builds: ; @object a b makes an object of its parts a and b,
and ; @array an array. Without them the value is the compiler\(aqs parse tree,
a {"rule", "src", "kids"} node for each rule.
.PP
A grammar reads its input as plain text: { } [ ] : , are ordinary characters, #
starts a comment to the end of the line, and a word is the token TX. NR, ST and
VL bring back numbers, quoted strings, and true, false and null.
.PP
A grammar that does not compile is refused before any input is read, as a usage
error. So is a repetition count over 1,024, or repetitions that would have the
compiler write more than 1,024 rules. An input the grammar does not accept
opens as a parse error, with the grammar\(aqs name as its format.
.PP
aless\(aqs source holds grammars for /etc/hosts, crontabs, /etc/passwd,
/etc/group, /etc/fstab, /etc/resolv.conf and KEY=value files, each with a
sample, and a guide to writing one:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
https://github.com/rjrodger/aless/tree/main/tests/fixtures/grammars
.fi
.RE
.SH "LARGE INPUTS AND LIMITS"
aless reads an input whole and parses it whole before it shows anything. A
parse runs at about a megabyte a second, and needs about 40 bytes of memory for
each byte of input. The viewer waits for it: before a large file is read aless
says so, and a reload of a large watched file pauses the viewer until the parse
ends.
.PP
\-\-max\-size (64M unless set) refuses a larger input before reading it, in a
tab that says so and names the size that would read it. \-\-timeout stops a
parse that runs too long, in a tab that says how far it got.
.PP
A document nested deeper than aless reads opens as a parse error with the code
too_deep. Every parse stops at 3,000 open rules, about 1,000 levels, and most
grammars stop sooner: at 127 levels for JSON, JSONL, jsonic, JSON5, YAML, TOML,
INI, ZON and expressions, 256 for XML, 512 for JSONC, and 768 open rules for
CSS. A .proto file\(aqs braces may nest 100 deep, counted before it is parsed.
Markdown\(aqs tree stops at 205 levels, without an error.
.PP
C stops at an expression of about 127 operations, 1,024 open rules or a syntax
tree 256 levels deep. Its tree repeats subtrees, so it also stops at a tree of
more than 256 values, or more than 2,048 bytes of names and strings, for each
byte of the file (between a million and 33,554,432 values, and between 8 MB and
256 MB). A grammar in ABNF, EBNF or GBNF may nest its groups and elements 127
deep.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP 7
\fB0\fR
the viewer was quit
.TP 7
\fB1\fR
the viewer failed while it ran: the terminal could not be drawn on or read
.TP 7
\fB2\fR
the viewer could not start: bad usage (an unknown option, a bad value, a
\-\-grammar that does not compile or cannot be read), or no terminal to draw on
and read keys from
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP 10
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
set and not empty: draw without colour, as \-\-no\-color does
.TP 10
\fBTERM\fR
dumb: a terminal that cannot draw the viewer, so aless refuses to start, as it
does without a terminal
.SH "FILES"
A release archive carries the man page and the completions, which \-\-generate
also prints:
.TP 18
\fBman/aless.1\fR
the man page (\-\-generate man)
.PD 0
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/aless.bash\fR
bash (\-\-generate complete\-bash)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/_aless\fR
zsh (\-\-generate complete\-zsh)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/aless.fish\fR
fish (\-\-generate complete\-fish)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/_aless.ps1\fR
PowerShell (\-\-generate complete\-powershell)
.PD
.PP
A shell reads its completions where it looks for them, which is where a package
puts them, or from its startup file each time it starts:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
eval "$(aless \-\-generate complete\-bash)"     # \(ti/.bashrc
eval "$(aless \-\-generate complete\-zsh)"      # \(ti/.zshrc, after compinit
aless \-\-generate complete\-fish | source      # config.fish
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.nf
aless config.yaml                      one file, in a tab
aless deploy.yaml values.toml a.csv    several files, a tab each
curl \-s https://api.example/x | aless  standard input, read as JSON
aless \-k yaml settings.conf            a file whose extension misleads
aless \-\-grammar hosts=hosts.abnf /etc/hosts
                                       a format of your own
aless \-\-no\-watch big.json              no reload when the file changes
aless \-m line \-n x.json                line mode, the rows numbered
aless                                  explore the current directory
.fi
